.TH "NTP.TOML" "5" "" "ntpd-rs 2.0.0-alpha.20261113" "ntpd-rs"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
\f[V]ntp.toml\f[R] - configuration file for the ntpd-rs statime-daemon
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The configuration of the ntpd-rs daemon is controlled through a number
of TOML configuration files.
The primary configuration file is loaded from
\f[V]/etc/ntpd-rs/ntp.toml\f[R], unless otherwise specified on the
command line when starting the daemon.
It can specify a folder containing further configuration files, which
are used to provide defaults for settings not present in the primary
configuration file.
.PP
The TOML file format is in lots of ways similar to a simple INI with
several extensions allowing a JSON-like syntax.
For the simplest cases, each setting is placed on its own line, with the
name of the setting on the left hand sign of an equals sign, and the
value on the right hand side.
String values are quoted, whilst numbers and boolean values like
true/false are placed without any quotes.
This document will provide example values for all settings.
A full specification of the syntax of toml files can be found at
toml.io (https://toml.io).
.PP
The ntpd-rs configuration is split up into several sections.
Each of these sections controls a separate aspect of the daemons
operations.
Most settings have defaults, which are shown in brackets as
(\f[B]emphasized\f[R]).
This document organizes all settings by section.
.SH EXPERIMENTAL FEATURES
.PP
The ntpd-rs daemon supports a number of features which are experimental.
Such features may change between versions in ways which are backwards
incompatible.
Settings related to such features will be marked in this documentation.
Note that a configuration using such settings may not work with future
versions of ntpd-rs.
.SH SPECIFICATION OF DURATIONS
.PP
Various configuration settings of the daemon set some kind of duration
or time interval.
These settings are specified as a human readable duration consisting of
a string with an amount and then a unit, such as
\f[V]\[dq]1.2s\[dq]\f[R].
The supported units for durations are nanoseconds\ (\f[V]ns\f[R]),
microseconds\ (\f[V]us\f[R]), milliseconds\ (\f[V]ms\f[R]),
seconds\ (\f[V]s\f[R]), minutes\ (\f[V]m\f[R]), hours\ (\f[V]h\f[R]),
days\ (\f[V]d\f[R]), and weeks\ (\f[V]w\f[R]).
.SH CONFIGURATION
.PP
There are two settings in the daemon which change its global behavior:
.TP
\f[V]use-system-config\f[R] = \f[I]bool or path\f[R] (\f[B]false\f[R])
Whether and where to fetch the system defaults.
If specified as \f[V]true\f[R], the system defaults are loaded from
/usr/lib/ntpd-rs/system-config.
The configuration files in the system config folder need to be disjunct,
i.e.\ a setting can be specified in at most one file.
In particular, this also holds for sources and servers, in that there
can be only one file specifying sources, and only one file specifying
servers.
.TP
\f[V]seccomp\f[R] = \f[I]mode\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]off\[rq]\f[R])
This setting is linux-specific.
Enables or disables the seccomp filter.
Can be set to \f[V]\[dq]off\[dq]\f[R], \f[V]\[dq]log\[dq]\f[R], or
\f[V]\[dq]kill\[dq]\f[R].
In log mode, seccomp violations are only logged, but not acted upon.
Note that the filter is experimental and may change in backwards
incompatible ways.
It also may miss system calls required by your specific libc.
Please test with logging before applying the filter in a stricter mode.
.SS \f[V][observability]\f[R]
.PP
Settings in this section configure how you can observe the behavior of
the daemon.
Currently, the daemon can be observed either through the logs or by
retrieving several key metrics either through statime(8) or through
ntp-metrics-exporter(8).
.TP
\f[V]log-level\f[R] = \f[V]\[dq]trace\[dq]\f[R] | \f[V]\[dq]debug\[dq]\f[R] | \f[V]\[dq]info\[dq]\f[R] | \f[V]\[dq]warn\[dq]\f[R] | \f[V]\[dq]error\[dq]\f[R] (\f[B]info\f[R])
Set the logging level for messages printed to stdout.
The lowest level \f[V]trace\f[R] gives very detailed information about
anything going on in the daemon, whereas the highest level
\f[V]error\f[R] only logs error conditions in the daemon.
Levels higher than the given log level are logged as well.
.TP
\f[V]log-path\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R] (\f[B]unset\f[R])
Path to which the daemon should direct its log output.
The file at this location is reopened on SIGHUP.
When not present, log output will be directed to stdout.
.TP
\f[V]log-path-metrics-exporter\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R] (\f[B]unset\f[R])
Path to which the metrics exporter daemon should direct its log output.
The file at this location is reopened on SIGHUP.
When not present, log output will be directed to stdout.
.TP
\f[V]ansi-colors\f[R] = \f[V]true\f[R] | \f[V]false\f[R] (see note)
Can be used to control ANSI escape codes in logs.
By default, ANSI escape codes are used to add some colors and other
formatting to the logs when the log target is a terminal.
.TP
\f[V]observation-path\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R] (\f[B]unset\f[R])
Path where the daemon will create an observation Unix domain socket.
This socket is used by \f[V]statime\f[R] and
\f[V]ntp-metrics-exporter\f[R] to read the current status of the daemon.
If not set (the default) no observation socket will be created and it is
not possible to use \f[V]statime\f[R] or \f[V]ntp-metrics-exporter\f[R]
to observe the daemon.
.TP
\f[V]observation-permissions\f[R] = \f[I]mode\f[R] (\f[B]0o666\f[R])
The file system permissions with which the observation socket should be
created.
Warning: You should always write this number with the octal prefix
\f[V]0o\f[R], otherwise your permissions might be interpreted wrongly.
The default should be OK for most applications however.
.TP
\f[V]metrics-exporter-listen\f[R] = \f[I]socketaddr\f[R] (\f[B]127.0.0.1:9975\f[R])
The listen address that is used for the ntp-metrics-exporter(8).
.SS \f[V][[source]]\f[R]
.PP
Each \f[V][[source]]\f[R] is a group of one or more time sources for the
daemon to retrieve time information from.
Any number of sources can be configured by repeating a
\f[V][[source]]\f[R] section (note the double brackets) for as many
times as required.
.PP
The daemon supports multiple ways of getting the time, from local
sources such as a gps receiver or a pps input, to remote sources such as
an NTP or csptp server.
The following source types are currently supported:
.TP
\f[V]ntp\f[R]
An NTP source connects to a single remote NTP server.
If the connection to the server is lost, attempts are made to reconnect
to the source unless the connection was secured and the server indicated
it never wanted to see the client again.
.TP
\f[V]ntp-pool\f[R]
An NTP pool source connects to several distinct NTP servers out of a
pool of NTP servers.
The pool is expected to either provide several servers behind the DNS
name of the pool, or to provide multiple sources via NTS.
Note that support for NTS with pool sources is still experimental.
If the connection to the server is lost, a new server is retrieved from
the pool.
.TP
\f[V]sock\f[R]
A sock source connects to a GPSd socket to get timing data from GPS
receivers.
Note that GPSd must be (re-)started after starting ntpd-rs for GPSd to
connect to the socket.
.TP
\f[V]pps\f[R]
A PPS source connects to a Pulse Per Second device, which is by default
assumed to send a pulse at the start of every second.
As these devices only provide periodic data, they do not count towards
\f[V]minimum-agreeing-sources\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]csptp\f[R]
A CSPTP source connects to a single csptp server.
If the connection to the server is lost, attempts are made to reconnect
to the source.
Note that CSPTP sources are still experimental, and only available on
linux.
.PP
A source has the following configuration options:
.TP
\f[V]type\f[R] = \f[I]type\f[R]
Specify one of the source types that ntpd-rs supports: \f[V]ntp\f[R],
\f[V]ntp-pool\f[R], \f[V]sock\f[R], \f[V]pps\f[R], or \f[V]csptp\f[R].
Note that \f[V]csptp\f[R] sources are experimental.
.TP
\f[V]nts\f[R] = \f[I]bool\f[R] (\f[B]false\f[R])
Whether to secure communication with the source using NTS.
Only supported for \f[V]ntp\f[R] and \f[V]ntp-pool\f[R] sources.
Note that using NTS with \f[V]ntp-pool\f[R] sources is experimental.
.TP
\f[V]address\f[R] = \f[I]address\f[R]
The address of the time source.
Only applicable to \f[V]ntp\f[R], \f[V]ntp-pool\f[R], and
\f[V]csptp\f[R] sources.
For non-NTS NTP sources, this should be the address of the NTP server.
For csptp servers, this should be the address of the csptp server.
For NTS sources, this should be the address of the NTS key exchange
server.
For pool sources, this should either be the dns name of the pool, or the
NTS key exhchange server of the pool when using NTS with the pool.
For NTP sources, the address may include a port number by appending a
colon\ (\f[V]:\f[R]) followed by a port number.
If not specified, the daemon will use the default port number
(\f[I]4460\f[R] if the source uses NTS, \f[I]123\f[R] if it does not).
.TP
\f[V]path\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R]
The path to the device or socket of the time source.
Only applicable to \f[V]pps\f[R] and \f[V]sock\f[R] sources.
.TP
\f[V]count\f[R] = \f[I]number\f[R] (\f[B]4\f[R])
Can only be set on \f[V]ntp-pool\f[R] sources.
Specifies the maximum number of servers that the daemon will attempt to
connect to from a pool.
The daemon will keep retrying to get more sources from the pool when
connections are lost, up to the maximum specified by this configuration
value.
.TP
\f[V]ignore\f[R] = \f[I]ip addresses\f[R] (\f[B][]\f[R])
Can only be set on \f[V]ntp-pool\f[R] sources not using NTS.
Specifies a list of IP addresses of servers in the pool which should not
be used.
For example: \f[V][\[dq]127.0.0.1\[dq]]\f[R].
Empty by default.
.TP
\f[V]ntp-version\f[R] = \f[V]4\f[R] | \f[V]5\f[R] | \f[V]\[dq]auto\[dq]\f[R] (\f[B]4\f[R])
Which NTP version to use for this source.
Can only be set on \f[V]ntp\f[R] and \f[V]ntp-pool\f[R] sources.
By default this uses NTP version 4.
You can use \f[V]5\f[R] to set the protocol version to the draft NTPv5
specification, but your source must support the same draft.
To use the experimental upgrade protocol set the NTP version to
\f[V]\[dq]auto\[dq]\f[R].
This uses a specially constructed NTPv4 packet to attempt an upgrade to
the supported draft NTPv5 version if the source also supports the same
draft version.
NTPv5 support is currently in beta and can still change in a backwards
incompatible way.
.TP
\f[V]minimum-poll-interval\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (see text)
Can only be set on \f[V]ntp\f[R] and \f[V]ntp-pool\f[R] sources.
The minimum amount of time between two requests to the server.
The value is given as a duration.
The default value is taken from the \f[V]minimum-poll-interval\f[R]
setting in \f[V][ntp-source-defaults]\f[R], which defaults to 16
seconds.
This is rounded up to the nearest power of 2 number of seconds.
.TP
\f[V]maximum-poll-interval\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (see text)
Can only be set on \f[V]ntp\f[R] and \f[V]ntp-pool\f[R] sources.
The maximum amount of time between two requests to the server.
The value is given as a duration.
The default value is taken from the \f[V]minimum-poll-interval\f[R]
setting in \f[V][ntp-source-defaults]\f[R], which defaults to 1024
seconds.
This is rounded down to the nearest power of 2 number of seconds.
If the rounded value of \f[V]minimum-poll-interval\f[R] is larger than
the \f[V]maximum-poll-interval\f[R], \f[V]maximum-poll-interval\f[R] is
taken equal to \f[V]minimum-poll-interval\f[R] instead.
.TP
\f[V]desired-error\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (see text)
The accuracy of synchronization desired from this source.
The actual poll interval is adapted between the limits specified in
\f[V]minimum-poll-interval\f[R] and \f[V]maximum-poll-interval\f[R] such
that the clock has this much uncertainty if only synchronized from this
source.
The default is taken from the \f[V]desired-error\f[R] setting in
\f[V][ntp-source-defaults]\f[R], which defaults to 1 millisecond.
.TP
\f[V]additional-certificates\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R] (optional)
Can only be set on sources using NTS.
Optional path to a certificate set for additional certicate authorities
to use, in addition to the certificate authorities specified by the
system configuration.
Note that this cannot be used to specify a self-signed certificate.
.TP
\f[V]precision\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R]
\f[V]pps\f[R] and \f[V]sock\f[R] sources only.
Precision of the source.
This should be an estimate of the size of the expected measurement
noise.
Technically defined as the 1-standard deviation bound on the measurement
error.
This is needed as \f[V]sock\f[R] and \f[V]pps\f[R] sources don\[cq]t
have a good way to estimate their own error.
.TP
\f[V]accuracy\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]0s\[rq]\f[R])
\f[V]pps\f[R] and \f[V]sock\f[R] sources only.
Accuracy of the underlying time source.
This should be an estimate of the size of the error in the clock you are
synchronizing with, as well as any mostly-unchanging offset in the
measurement process.
This can be used to deprioritize sources which have large offsets in the
measurement process or which are of poorer quality than others.
.TP
\f[V]period\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]1s\[rq]\f[R])
Only for \f[V]pps\f[R] sources.
Period of the pulse signal.
The time signal is assumed to arive on integer multiples of the period,
starting at the (virtual) TAI epoch of midnight on the first of januari
1970.
Note that this means that when using multi-second periods, these may not
align with the minute or hour mark of the UTC timescale.
.TP
\f[V]enable-srv-resolution\f[R] = \f[I]bool\f[R] (\f[B]false\f[R])
Can only be set on sources using NTS.
Enables following SRV records when resolving the address of the NTS key
exchange server.
Such records must be DNSSEC signed.
Note that this feature is experimental and may change in backwards
incompatible ways.
.TP
\f[V]delay-decay-rate\f[R] = \f[I]rate\f[R] (see text)
Can only be set for \f[V]ntp\f[R], \f[V]ntp-pool\f[R] and
\f[V]csptp\f[R] sources.
How quickly we lose confidence in the observed time delay between us and
this time source.
This setting is experimental and primarily intended for use by experts
when fine-tuning the synchronization algorithm.
The default value is taken from the \f[V]delay-decay-rate\f[R] setting
in \f[V][ntp-source-defaults]\f[R] for NTP sources, and from the
\f[V]delay-decay-rate\f[R] setting in \f[V][csptp-source-defaults]\f[R]
for \f[V]csptp\f[R] sources, both of which defaults to
\f[V]0.003402069\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]domain\f[R] = \f[I]number\f[R] (see text)
Can only be set on \f[V]csptp\f[R] sources.
PTP domain number to be used in communication with the CSPTP server.
The default value is taken from the \f[V]domain\f[R] setting in
\f[V][csptp-source-defaults]\f[R], which defaults to 128.
.TP
\f[V]poll-interval\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (see text)
Can only be set on \f[V]csptp\f[R] sources.
How often to query the CSPTP server for the current time.
The default value is taken from the \f[V]poll-interval\f[R] setting in
\f[V][csptp-source-defaults]\f[R], which defaults to 1 second.
.TP
\f[V]response-interval\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (see text)
Can only be set on \f[V]csptp\f[R] sources.
How long to wait for a reply to a request.
Replies received after this interval will be ignored.
The default value is taken from the \f[V]response-interval\f[R] setting
in \f[V][csptp-source-defaults]\f[R], which defaults to 1 second.
.SS \f[V][filter]\f[R]
.PP
Before being used to synchronize the system\[cq]s time, sources are
checked for sufficient quality and against each other for consistency.
These settings control how that checking is done:
.TP
\f[V]minimum-agreeing-sources\f[R] = \f[I]number\f[R] (see text)
The number of sources that need to agree on the current time before the
current system\[cq]s time is modified.
By default, this is the smaller of 3 and the number of sources
configured, excluding any pulse-per-second sources.
.TP
\f[V]select-offset-uncertainty-window\f[R] = \f[I]size\f[R] (\f[B]1.0\f[R])
Size of the window of uncertainty to assume around a source\[cq]s
offset, based on the observed noise in the offset.
This setting is experimental and may change in backwards incompatible
ways.
It is primarily intended for experts trying to tune the algorithm.
.TP
\f[V]select-link-uncertainty-window\f[R] = \f[I]size\f[R] (\f[B]2.0\f[R])
Size of the window of uncertainty to assume around a source\[cq]s
offset, based on the observed noise in the link delay.
This setting is experimental and may change in backwards incompatible
ways.
It is primarily intended for experts trying to tune the algorithm.
.TP
\f[V]select-delay-uncertainty-window\f[R] = \f[I]size\f[R] (\f[B]0.5\f[R])
Size of the window of uncertainty to assume around a source\[cq]s
offset, based on the potential asymmetry due to delay and root delay.
This setting is experimental and may change in backwards incompatible
ways.
It is primarily intended for experts trying to tune the algorithm.
.TP
\f[V]select-max-window-size\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]1s\f[R])
Maximum total size of the uncertainty window of a source before we judge
a source to be unsuitable for synchronization.
This setting is experimental and may change in backwards incompatible
ways.
It is primarily intended for experts trying to tune the algorithm.
.SS \f[V][system-clock]\f[R]
.PP
Controls for how the system clock is steered:
.TP
\f[V]steered\f[R] = \f[I]bool\f[R] (\f[B]true\f[R])
Whether the system clock is actually steered.
If false, the daemon keeps track of the deviation of the system clock
from the actual time, but the actual system clock is not updated based
on that estimate.
.TP
\f[V]startup-jump-warning-threshold\f[R] = \f[I]threshold set\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]0s\[rq]\f[R])
How large a step must be at startup to trigger a warning.
Can be set to \[lq]inf\[rq] to disable this warning entirely.
The threshold can be either symmetric, in which case it is specified as
a duration, or asymmetric, in which case a separate forward and backward
jump threshold can be given, each of which can be infinite.
An example of an asymmetric threshold is
\f[V]{ forward = \[dq]inf\[dq], backward = \[dq]0s\[dq] }\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]startup-jump-abort-threshold\f[R] = \f[I]threshold set\f[R] (\f[B]{ forward = \[lq]inf\[rq], backward = \[lq]1d\[rq] }\f[R])
How large a step must be at startup to cause us to stop trusting our
ability to steer the clock and abort the daemon.
Can be set to \[lq]inf\[rq] to disable aborting on jumps at startup
entirely.
The threshold can be either symmetric, in which case it is specified as
a duration, or asymmetric, in which case a separate forward and backward
jump threshold can be given, each of which can be infinite.
.TP
\f[V]single-jump-warning-threshold\f[R] = \f[I]threshold set\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]0s\[rq]\f[R])
How large a jump during regular operation must be to trigger a warning.
Can be set to \[lq]inf\[rq] to disable this warning entirely.
The threshold can be either symmetric, in which case it is specified as
a duration, or asymmetric, in which case a separate forward and backward
jump threshold can be given, each of which can be infinite.
An example of an asymmetric threshold is
\f[V]{ forward = \[dq]inf\[dq], backward = \[dq]0s\[dq] }\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]single-jump-abort-threshold\f[R] = \f[I]threshold set\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]1000s\[rq]\f[R])
How large a jump must be during regular operation to cause us to stop
trusting our ability to steer the clock and abort the daemon.
Can be set to \[lq]inf\[rq] to disable aborting on jumps during regular
operations entirely.
The threshold can be either symmetric, in which case it is specified as
a duration, or asymmetric, in which case a separate forward and backward
jump threshold can be given, each of which can be infinite.
An example of an asymmetric threshold is
\f[V]{ forward = \[dq]inf\[dq], backward = \[dq]0s\[dq] }\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]accumulated-jump-warning-threshold\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]inf\[rq]\f[R])
How large all jumps, excluding jumps at startup, combined must be,
befores triggering a warning.
Can be used to trigger a warning before an abort is triggered from
passing \f[V]accumulated-jump-abort-threshold\f[R], giving the operator
a chance to intervene before that threshold is triggered.
Can be set to \[lq]inf\[rq] to disable this warning entirely.
.TP
\f[V]accumulated-jump-abort-threshold\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]inf\[rq]\f[R])
How large all jumps, excluding a single jump at startup, combined must
be, before we stop trusting our ability to steer the clock and abort the
daemon.
Can be set to \[lq]inf\[rq] to disable aborting on the total amount
jumped entirely.
.TP
\f[V]startup-end-threshold\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]1s\[rq]\f[R])
The uncertainty on the current time below which we consider the clock to
have transitioned out of startup.
If the clock later goes to a larger uncertainty again, it will still be
considered out of startup.
This setting is experimental and may change in backwards incompatible
ways.
It is primarily intended for experts trying to tune the algorithm.
.TP
\f[V]jump-threshold\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]10s\[rq]\f[R])
How large the clock offset needs to be before we make a jump.
This setting is experimental and may change in backwards incompatible
ways.
It is primarily intended for experts trying to tune the algorithm.
.TP
\f[V]slew-time-constant\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]8s\[rq]\f[R])
The amount of time to spread slewing over.
This gives a typical time scale to reduce the offset error by about two
thirds using slews.
This setting is experimental and may change in backwards incompatible
ways.
It is primarily intended for experts trying to tune the algorithm.
.TP
\f[V]synchronized-threshold\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]1s\[rq]\f[R])
Offset and uncertainty below which we consider the clock synchronized.
This setting is experimental and may change in backwards incompatible
ways.
It is primarily intended for experts trying to tune the algorithm.
.TP
\f[V]initial-wander\f[R] = \f[I]amount\f[R] (\f[B]1e-8\f[R])
Initial estimate of how much the frequency of the clock wanders, as
fractions of a second per second.
This setting is experimental and may change in backwards incompatible
ways.
It is primarily intended for experts trying to tune the algorithm.
.SS \f[V][ntp-source-defaults]\f[R]
.PP
Some of the behavior of NTP sources is configurable.
The defaults for these settings can be set here:
.TP
\f[V]minimum-poll-interval\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]16s\[rq]\f[R])
The minimum amount of time between two requests to the NTP server.
The value is given as a duration.
This is rounded up to the nearest power of 2 number of seconds.
.TP
\f[V]maximum-poll-interval\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]1024s\[rq]\f[R])
The maximum amount of time between two requests to the NTP server.
The value is given as a duration.
This is rounded down to the nearest power of 2 number of seconds.
If the rounded value of \f[V]minimum-poll-interval\f[R] is larger than
the \f[V]maximum-poll-interval\f[R], \f[V]maximum-poll-interval\f[R] is
taken equal to \f[V]minimum-poll-interval\f[R] instead.
.TP
\f[V]desired-error\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]1ms\[rq]\f[R])
The accuracy of synchronization desired from NTP sources.
The actual poll interval is adapted between the limits specified in
\f[V]minimum-poll-interval\f[R] and \f[V]maximum-poll-interval\f[R] such
that the clock has this much uncertainty if only synchronized from this
source.
.TP
\f[V]delay-decay-rate\f[R] = \f[I]rate\f[R] (\f[B]0.003402069\f[R])
How quickly we lose confidence in the observed time delay between us and
NTP time sources.
This setting is experimental and primarily intended for use by experts
when fine-tuning the synchronization algorithm.
.SS \f[V][csptp-source-defaults]\f[R]
.PP
Some of the behaviour of csptp sources is configurable.
The defaults for these settings can be set here.
Note that csptp sources are still experimental, and may change in
backwards incompatible ways in future versions.
.TP
\f[V]domain\f[R] = \f[I]number\f[R] (\f[B]128\f[R])
PTP domain number to be used in communication with CSPTP servers.
.TP
\f[V]poll-interval\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]1s\[rq]\f[R])
How often to query the CSPTP server for the current time.
.TP
\f[V]response-interval\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]1s\[rq]\f[R])
How long to wait for a reply to a request.
Replies received after this interval will be ignored.
.TP
\f[V]delay-decay-rate\f[R] = \f[I]rate\f[R] (\f[B]0.003402069\f[R])
How quickly we lose confidence in the observed time delay between us and
csptp time sources.
This setting is experimental and primarily intended for use by experts
when fine-tuning the synchronization algorithm.
.SS \f[V][[server]]\f[R]
.PP
The daemon can be configured to act as a server for various protocol via
one or more \f[V][[server]]\f[R] sections.
If no such sections have been defined, the daemon runs in a client only
mode.
Each server listens on a specific socket address and port, for a
specific protocol.
The following server types are currently supported:
.TP
\f[V]ntp\f[R]
A server providing time to clients via the NTP protocol, optionally
protected with NTS.
Can be configured to support versions 3, 4, and drafts of version 5.
Note that support for NTP version 5 drafts is experimental and may
change in backwards incompatible ways.
.TP
\f[V]ntske\f[R]
A server handling NTS key exchange requests for clients, providing the
initial setup needed for protecting NTP exchanges with NTS.
.TP
\f[V]csptp\f[R]
A server providing time to clients via a draft version of the csptp
protocol.
Note that support for csptp is experimental and may change in backwards
incompatible ways.
.PP
A source has the following configuration options:
.TP
\f[V]type\f[R] = \f[I]server type\f[R]
Specify one of the server types ntpd-rs supports.
Must be one of \f[V]ntp\f[R], \f[V]ntske\f[R], or \f[V]csptp\f[R].
Note that \f[V]csptp\f[R] servers are experimental and may change in
backwards incompatible ways.
.TP
\f[V]listen\f[R] = \f[I]socket address\f[R]
Address of the socket on which the server should listen for incoming
requests.
Is interpreted as a UDP socket address for \f[V]ntp\f[R] servers, and as
a TCP socket address for \f[V]ntske\f[R] servers.
Cannot be specified for \f[V]csptp\f[R] servers.
Specified as an interface IP address, a colon, and a port number.
When the port number is not specified, UDP port 123 will be used for
\f[V]ntp\f[R] servers, and TCP port 4460 will be used for
\f[V]ntske\f[R] servers.
Both IPv4 and IPv6 are supported.
It is possible to listen on all addresses associated with the local
machine using \f[V]0.0.0.0:123\f[R] for IPv4, or \f[V][::]:123\f[R] for
IPv6.
.TP
\f[V]accept-ntp-version\f[R] = \f[I]list of NTP versions\f[R] (see text)
A list of NTP versions this server should support.
Cannot be specified for \f[V]csptp\f[R] servers.
By default, \f[V]ntp\f[R] servers will support NTP versions 3 and 4
(corresponding to the configuration value\f[V][3, 4]\f[R]), whilst
\f[V]ntske\f[R] servers will only support NTP version 4.
\f[V]ntske\f[R] servers cannot support NTP version 3 as NTS requires NTP
version 4 or later.
Support for a draft of NTP version 5 can be enabled by including 5 in
the list of accepted NTP versions.
Note that NTP version 5 support is experimental and may change in
backwards incompatible ways.
.TP
\f[V]require-nts\f[R] = \f[I]bool or response action\f[R] (\f[B]false\f[R])
For \f[V]ntp\f[R] servers only.
Whether to only respond to NTS secured NTP packets.
When set to true, incoming NTP packets that are not authenticated are
silently ignored.
Can also be set to \f[V]deny\f[R] to respond with a NTP packet
indicating the client is not welcome, or to \f[V]ignore\f[R] to
explicitly ignore non-NTS NTP packets.
.TP
\f[V]allowlist\f[R] = { ranges = [ \f[I]subnet\f[R], .. ], action = \f[V]\[dq]deny\[dq]\f[R] | \f[V]\[dq]ignore\[dq]\f[R] } (\f[B]unset\f[R])
For \f[V]ntp\f[R] servers only.
Only allow any number of filtered \f[I]subnets\f[R] to connect to the
server.
Any IP that matches one of the subnets specified is allowed to contact
this server.
The subnets must be specified in CIDR notation (an IP address followed
by a slash and the number of masked bits, for example
\f[V]127.0.0.1/8\f[R] or \f[V]192.168.1.1/24\f[R]).
The action determines what measure is taken for IP addresses not in any
of the specified subnets.
When \f[V]deny\f[R], an explicit packet with the NTP \f[V]DENY\f[R] kiss
code is returned to the sender indicating that they are not allowed to
do so.
When \f[V]ignore\f[R] is specified, messages are discarded with no
response sent.
The default value is equivalent to allowing any IP address, and would be
equivalent to setting the filter to
\f[V][\[dq]0.0.0.0/0\[dq], \[dq]::/0\[dq]]\f[R], with either action.
.TP
\f[V]denylist\f[R] = { ranges = [ \f[I]subnet\f[R], .. ], action = \f[V]\[dq]deny\[dq]\f[R] | \f[V]\[dq]ignore\[dq]\f[R] } (\f[B]unset\f[R])
For \f[V]ntp\f[R] servers only.
Do not allow any number of filtered \f[I]subnets\f[R] to connect to the
daemon.
Any IP that matches one of the subnets specified is not allowed to
contact this server.
The subnets must be specified in CIDR notation (an IP address followed
by a slash and the number of masked bits, for example
\f[V]127.0.0.1/8\f[R] or \f[V]192.168.1.1/24\f[R]).
The action determines what measure is taken for IP addresses in any of
the specified subnets.
When \f[V]deny\f[R], an explicit packet with the NTP \f[V]DENY\f[R] kiss
code is returned to the sender indicating that they are not allowed to
do so.
When \f[V]ignore\f[R] is specified, messages are discarded with no
response sent.
The default value is equivalent to allowing any IP address, and would be
equivalent to setting the filter to \f[V][]\f[R], with either action.
.TP
\f[V]rate-limiting-cache-size\f[R] = \f[I]size\f[R] (\f[B]0\f[R])
For \f[V]ntp\f[R] servers only.
Number of elements in the rate limiting cache.
At most \f[I]size\f[R] elements are kept in the cache.
This means that if more than \f[I]size\f[R] different clients attempt to
connect to the server too frequently, the cache will have reduced
functionality, as rate limiting information gets lost when new clients
connect to the server.
If set to zero, the cache is unused and rate limiting is disabled, this
is the default.
.TP
\f[V]rate-limiting-cutoff-ms\f[R] = \f[I]cutoff\f[R] (\f[B]0\f[R])
For \f[V]ntp\f[R] servers only.
Minimum time between two requests from the same client, if a request was
sent sooner than the cutoff time, the client will be asked to slow down
their requests by the server responding with a packet with the NTP
\f[V]RATE\f[R] kiss code.
No actual time measurement will be returned to the client in that case.
If set to zero, no rate limiting is applied, this is the default.
.TP
\f[V]certificate-chain\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R]
For \f[V]ntske\f[R] servers only.
Path to a certificate chain for the public certificate that the server
offers to clients.
.TP
\f[V]private-key\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R]
For \f[V]ntske\f[R] servers only.
Path to the private key associated with the server certificate in the
certificate chain.
.PP
\f[V]ntp-port\f[R] = \f[I]port\f[R] For \f[V]ntske\f[R] servers only.
Port number the key exchange server should instruct clients to use.
Should be used when the port number of the NTP server is not the
default.
.PP
\f[V]ntp-server\f[R] = \f[I]server-name\f[R] For \f[V]ntske\f[R] servers
only.
Server address (either as ip or as domain name) where clients can find
the NTP server.
Should be used when this name does not match the name of the NTS key
exchange server.
.TP
\f[V]key-exchange-timeout-ms\f[R] = \f[I]timeout\f[R] (\f[B]1000\f[R])
For \f[V]ntske\f[R] servers only.
Timeout in milliseconds for how long a key exchange may take.
If the timeout is exceeded the connection will be dropped.
.TP
\f[V]concurrent-connections\f[R] = \f[I]number\f[R] (\f[B]512\f[R])
For \f[V]ntske\f[R] servers only.
Maximum number of total concurrent connections the key exchange server
will handle.
Any connections above the threshold will be held in an OS level queue.
.TP
\f[V]network-wait-period\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]1s\[rq]\f[R])
For \f[V]ntp\f[R] and \f[V]ntske\f[R] servers only.
How long to wait before trying to reopen the network socket should the
network socket fail in some way.
This setting is experimental and its implementation may change in
backwards incompatible ways.
.TP
\f[V]accepted-pool-authentication-tokens\f[R] = \f[I]list of keys\f[R] (\f[B][]\f[R])
Tokens pools can use to authenticate pool-specific requests to this NTS
server.
Note that pool support is currently in beta and can still change in a
backwards incompatible way.
.TP
\f[V]longlived-connections\f[R] = \f[I]number\f[R] (\f[B]\f[VB]concurrent-connections\f[B]/10\f[R])
For \f[V]ntske\f[R] servers only.
Maximum number of concurrent longlived connections from NTS pools the
key exchange server will handle.
Note that pool support is currently in beta and can still change in
backwards incompatible ways.
.TP
\f[V]protocol\f[R] = \f[I]transport protocol\f[R]
For \f[V]csptp\f[R] servers only.
Which transport protocol to use for the csptp messages.
Can be either \f[V]\[dq]udpv4\[dq]\f[R], for udp transport over ipv4, or
\f[V]\[dq]udpv6\[dq]\f[R], for udp transport of ipv6.
Note that \f[V]csptp\f[R] support is experimental and cna still change
in backwards incompatible ways.
.SS \f[V][nts-cookie-keys]\f[R]
.PP
In NTS secured NTP traffic, the client communicates to the server which
keys to use through a cookie which it received from the server at an
earlier time.
In ntpd-rs, these cookies contain encrypted versions of the NTS keys.
The settings here control the generation and storage of these keys when
acting as an NTP secured NTS server or NTS key exchange server.
.TP
\f[V]old-key-count\f[R] = \f[I]count\f[R] (\f[B]7\f[R])
Maximum number of old keys to keep around.
Whenever keys are rotated the old keys will stop being used to generate
new cookies, but clients may still have NTS cookies encrypted with any
of the old keys.
.TP
\f[V]key-rotation-interval\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]1d\[rq]\f[R])
Time between key rotation events.
Every time \f[I]duration\f[R] elapses, a new internal key will be
generated for creating NTS cookies.
By default this is set to a day.
.TP
\f[V]storage-path\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R] (\f[B]unset\f[R])
If set, stores the internal NTS keys in the file indicated by
\f[I]path\f[R].
This allows keys to survive a server reboot.
If not set, clients using NTS may need to redo a key exchange operation
to get new NTS cookies.
The daemon will not create any parent directories if they don\[cq]t
exist.
The daemon will create the file if it doesn\[cq]t exist.
.SS \f[V][csptp]\f[R]
.PP
CSPTP servers provide some information to their clients about where the
time they provide comes from.
The settings in this section provide the values used for this when the
server isn\[cq]t synchronizing itself to another CSPTP time source.
Note that CSPTP support is based on a draft and still experimental, so
these settings may change in backwards incompatible ways.
.TP
\f[V]identity\f[R] = \f[I]clock identity\f[R] (\f[B][0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]\f[R])
The identity advertised to remote clocks when we are not synchronized to
another CSPTP time source.
.TP
\f[V]priority-1\f[R] = \f[I]number\f[R] (\f[B]255\f[R])
The value of priority-1 advertised to remote clocks when we are not
synchronized to another CSPTP time source.
.TP
\f[V]priority-2\f[R] = \f[I]number\f[R] (\f[B]255\f[R])
The value of priority-2 advertised to remote clocks when we are not
synchronized to another CSPTP time source.
.TP
\f[V]clock-class\f[R] = \f[I]number\f[R] (\f[B]248\f[R])
The clock class advertised to remote clocks when we are not synchronized
to another CSPTP time source.
For the meaning of this value, see table 4 in IEEE Std.
1588-2019.
.TP
\f[V]advertised-clock-accuracy\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]unset\f[R])
The accuracy of our clock advertised to remote clocks when we are not
synchronized to another CSPTP time source.
When not set, the accuracy is advertised as unknown.
.TP
\f[V]advertised-clock-precision\f[R] = \f[I]duration\f[R] (\f[B]\[lq]300us\[rq]\f[R])
The precision of our clock advertised to remote clocks when we are not
synchronized to another CSPTP time source.
.TP
\f[V]ptp-timescale\f[R] = \f[I]bool\f[R] (\f[B]true\f[R])
Whether we use the ptp timescale for the local clock.
If false, this implies an arbitrary timescale.
.TP
\f[V]time-traceable\f[R] = \f[I]bool\f[R] (\f[B]false\f[R])
Whether our local time is traceable to a valid standard reference when
we are not synchronized to another CSPTP time source.
.TP
\f[V]frequency-traceable\f[R] = \f[I]bool\f[R] (\f[B]false\f[R])
Whether our local clock frequency is traceable to a valid standard
reference when we are not synchronized to another CSPTP time source.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
statime-daemon(8), statime(8), ntp-metrics-exporter(8)
